<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Macbeth, King of Scotland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1005–<a href="/years/1057">1057</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Mac Bethad mac Findlaích</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Macbeth, in Gaelic Mac Bethad mac Findlaích, was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> from 1040 to 1057. Mormaer of Moray, he killed King Duncan I in battle in 1040 and took the throne, ruled securely enough to make a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1050, and was killed by Duncan's son Malcolm III at Lumphanan in 1057. Shakespeare's tragedy turned him into a byword for murderous ambition.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Macbeth's early life. He was the son of Findláech of Moray, who had been killed by Gille Coemgáin, and may have been a grandson of Malcolm II, presumably through the king's daughter Donada. His name, Mac Bethad, means son of life, and implies a righteous or religious man. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a> came north in 1031 to accept the submission of Malcolm II, Macbeth also submitted to him.</p><p>He became Mormaer of Moray, a semi-autonomous province, in 1032, and was probably responsible for the death of Gille Coemgáin. He then married Gille Coemgáin's widow, Gruoch, who may herself have had a claim to the throne. They had no children together, and Macbeth accepted her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lulach">Lulach</a> as his heir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Duncan I was acclaimed king of Alba on 30 November 1034, apparently without opposition. In 1039 the Northumbrians attacked Strathclyde and a retaliatory raid by Duncan against Durham ended in disaster. The next year he led an army into Moray, apparently on a punitive expedition, and was killed in action at Bothnagowan, near Elgin, by the men of Moray led by Macbeth, probably on 14 August 1040. Macbeth became king and no resistance to his accession is known.</p><p>In 1045 Duncan's father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crinan-of-dunkeld">Crínán of Dunkeld</a> was killed in a battle between two Scottish armies, and Duncan's brother Maldred is believed to have died there too, fighting Macbeth in defence of Duncan's young son Máel Coluim, later Malcolm III. Marianus Scotus records that Macbeth made a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1050 and gave money to the poor as if it were seed.</p><p>In 1052 he received Norman exiles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> at his court. In 1054 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siward-earl-of-northumbria">Siward, Earl of Northumbria</a>, led a very large invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> report 3,000 Scots and 1,500 English dead in the bloody battle at Dunsinane. Macbeth did not survive the English invasion for long: he was defeated and mortally wounded or killed by the future Malcolm III at Lumphanan in 1057. The Prophecy of Berchán says he died at Scone some days later. His stepson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lulach">Lulach</a> was installed as king but was soon killed by Malcolm.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Unlike later writers, no near-contemporary source calls Macbeth a tyrant. The Duan Albanach calls him Mac Bethad of fame, and the Prophecy of Berchán calls him the generous king of Fortriu. His life had moved far towards legend by the end of the 14th century, when John of Fordun and Andrew of Wyntoun wrote. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-shakespeare">William Shakespeare</a>'s tragedy, probably first performed in 1606 and based on Holinshed's Chronicles, is not historically accurate. A likely reason for its unflattering portrait is that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI and I</a> descended from Malcolm III, while Macbeth's line died out with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lulach">Lulach</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Macbeth, King of Scotland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Siward, Earl of Northumbria, invading Scotland in support of Malcolm Canmore's claim to the throne, defeated <b>Macbeth, King of Scotland</b>, somewhere north of the Firth of Forth.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-27">27 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Later Scottish genealogies traced the rulers of the Kingdom of Moray, including <b>Macbeth</b>, to this kindred, though the link is debated.</blockquote><a href="/state/cenel-loairn">Cenel Loairn</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Macbeth</b>, mormaer of Moray, killed Duncan I in 1040 and ruled Scotland until 1057, making a pilgrimage to Rome.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-moray">Kingdom of Moray</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Lumphanan, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=57.13,-2.69&amp;year=1038" aria-label="Lumphanan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4492 226 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Macbeth, King of Scotland"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4942 496h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4942 496h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=57.13,-2.69&amp;year=1038">What was here in 1038? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
